 ## Based on the honest out-of-sample evaluation using nested cross-validation (5-fold with 3 repeats), Random Forest proved to be the best-performing model, achieving the lowest test error with an expected MSE of 0.7489 (compared to the "predict-the-mean" baseline MSE of 0.9746 and OLS MSE of 0.8529). Random Forest excelled primarily because its bagging ensemble architecture and random feature subsampling (max_features=0.4, min_samples_leaf=3) naturally dampen individual tree variance and effectively capture non-linear relationships without memorizing the noise inherent in our small training sample (n = 316). In contrast, unpenalized OLS severely overfit the 46 post-encoding features, while standalone trees and regularized linear models (Ridge/Lasso at MSE ≈ 0.785 - 0.787) lacked either stability or the ability to exploit non-linear feature interactions. Consequently, the tuned Random Forest model was refit on the full training dataset to generate the final 79 predictions for test.rds.
